WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.400
I'm sitting here with Jacques one of
our lead front end web developers.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:04.900
Hi.
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:05.900
&gt;&gt; So

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.130
I was talking with Nick about
architecting a little bit.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:13.462
You know, like you've got some code
it's not necessarily up to snuff and

00:00:13.462 --> 00:00:16.863
you know you just move things around a
little bit to get it up to snuff right?

00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:21.740
So what if I were to give you a piece
of code that's like that but worse.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.430
&gt;&gt; You could sort of still tease
parts of it, you know, around and,

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.420
and sort of get into the modules or the
separations that you're interested in

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.850
and then replace those pieces as,
as you work on them.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.640
So, if the, if the code's not
stuff that you want to keep around

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.220
separating it out means you get to
burn little pieces of it at a time and

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.190
replace them and upgrade them and
make them, make them better.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.680
&gt;&gt; That makes sense.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.540
So what if I give you some code
that's even worse than that?

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.510
Like, let's just say,
big mess of spaghetti code.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.160
&gt;&gt; If you can't tease it apart, or

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.220
if there aren't good ideas that you'd
like to keep just burn it to the ground.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.060
&gt;&gt; Just start.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:06.130
&gt;&gt; Just take it all away and
rebuild that functionality.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.620
&gt;&gt; Just like that.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:08.150
Wow!

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:08.650
Okay.

00:01:09.822 --> 00:01:14.051
So I guess you're giving me permission
to burn horrible spaghetti code to

00:01:14.051 --> 00:01:14.810
the ground?

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:15.440
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.990
You will thank yourself in the future.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.105
What if it's worse than that?

00:01:19.105 --> 00:01:23.348
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Nuke it from space.

00:01:23.348 --> 00:01:24.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Have,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:25.875
have a friend delete it for you.

00:01:25.875 --> 00:01:28.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Don't even,
don't even look at it.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.260
&gt;&gt; No, no.
&gt;&gt; Just, okay, so, wonderful.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.580
Now you have permission
to delete horrible,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.450
awful spaghetti code from your projects.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.450
Huh, so you,
you actually have done this?

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.090
&gt;&gt; Yeah, oh, absolutely.
&gt;&gt; Have,

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.719
have you ever nuked
something from space?

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.650
Between Nick and I, we,
we have done that absolutely like,

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:50.024
hey could you get rid of all of that,
I'll, I'll replace it.

00:01:50.024 --> 00:01:50.785
&gt;&gt; Oh, boy.

00:01:50.785 --> 00:01:53.830
[LAUGH] Fantastic or horrifying.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:54.880
&gt;&gt; Or, or terrible, yeah.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:55.410
&gt;&gt; Terrible.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.250
&gt;&gt; kind of both.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:56.870
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.660
Well, thanks for sitting with me and
talking about this.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:01:59.160
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

